# Fork architecture

This repo is a **fork** of upstream NodeTool. Upstream is pulled regularly. Custom features, UI tweaks, and business logic must sit on top of base code so merges stay clean.

## Git remotes and publishing (mandatory)

This workspace always publishes to **one** fork remote. Do not push elsewhere.

| Remote | URL | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `origin` | `https://cgshortcuts@github.com/cgshortcuts/aistudio.git` | **Push / PR target** (AiStudio fork) |
| `upstream` | `https://github.com/nodetool-ai/nodetool.git` | Fetch-only NodeTool source |

Rules for agents and humans:

- This repo always authenticates as **`cgshortcuts`**, even when another GitHub CLI account is active (`maverickmongoose`, `dave-cgshortcuts`, etc.). Do **not** run `gh auth switch` for this workspace — that changes the machine-wide account and breaks other repos.
- Never change `origin` to another user/org unless the user explicitly asks.
- Sync from upstream with `npm run sync:upstream` (fetch) or `npm run sync:upstream -- --merge` (merge). Do not force-push to `upstream`.
- Re-pin remotes and the local credential helper anytime with `npm run git:ensure-remotes`.

## Where custom code lives

- Put all fork-only code under **`web/src/custom/`** (UI / web) or **`electron/src/custom/`** (desktop shell).
- Group by feature: `web/src/custom/<feature-name>/` with an `index.ts` barrel.
- Prefer new files in `custom/` over editing upstream files.

## Do not edit core unless required

Avoid changing upstream files for feature work. Allowed core touches are thin **mount points** only, for example:

- Importing and rendering a custom wrapper
- Registering a route, plugin, or hook
- One-line prop / composition seams

If a core edit is required, wrap it in comment boundaries:

```tsx
// === CUSTOM FORK START: [Feature Name] ===
import { ExpandableRailToolbar } from "../../custom/rail-hover-labels";
// === CUSTOM FORK END ===
```

```tsx
{/* === CUSTOM FORK START: [Feature Name] === */}
<ExpandableRailToolbar {...props} />
{/* === CUSTOM FORK END === */}
```

Use the same feature name in START and in the `custom/<feature>/` folder.

## Preferred extension patterns

- **Wrappers** — wrap or replace a base component from `custom/`; mount the wrapper at one core call site.
- **Registries / maps** — register nodes, routes, menu items, or panels from `custom/` instead of patching lists in core.
- **Hooks / events** — put fork behavior in custom hooks; call them from a marked mount point.
- **Style overlays** — keep fork CSS/emotion in `custom/`; do not permanently rewrite upstream style blocks when an overlay works.

## Forbidden patterns

- Large feature logic left inline inside upstream components
- Unmarked edits scattered across core files
- Copy-paste forks of whole upstream modules when a wrapper will do
- Editing upstream design tokens / constants for fork-only values — keep those under `custom/` (and ignore `src/custom/**` in design-token lint when needed)

## Checklist before finishing a fork change

1. New behavior lives under `web/src/custom/<feature>/` (or equivalent).
2. Core diffs are minimal and wrapped in `CUSTOM FORK` markers.
3. Tests for the feature live next to the custom module (`custom/<feature>/__tests__/`).
4. Upstream files still look upstream outside the marked mount points.
5. Pushes go only to `origin` → `cgshortcuts/aistudio` as account `cgshortcuts`.
